{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgArzFnfyU1BApzSutF4D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1305,"cache_read_input_tokens":35044,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1305},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"733d1f61-b9aa-4769-a690-e182734fcdea","uuid":"65a3fbfc-86b8-4b50-9682-c83a86a71411","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:10:53.733Z","request_id":"req_011CgArzFWYtsjXFRHMH4n5L","thinking_duration_ms":7650,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgArzFnfyU1BApzSutF4D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MVgWZwTSLydJ3cDbCVzQyX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-GiJWSF/ANSWER.md","content":"# notes-mcp: what real MCP clients will trip over\n\nChecked live against `python3 server.py` on port 56521 (curl, plus a read of `server.py`). I changed nothing in the server.\n\n## Disclosure\n\nWhile testing I called `add_note` twice with the dev token from README.md. The first call created `n-6`, and the second was a missing-argument check. Both went to the in-memory store only. `notes.json` is untouched, `git status` is clean, and I stopped the server afterwards. I should have kept to read-only calls.\n\n## Failures, most important first\n\n1. **Tool refusals are JSON-RPC errors, not tool results.**\n   - `get_note {}` returns `error -32602 \"missing required argument: id\"`.\n   - `get_note {\"id\":\"zz\"}` returns `error -32602 \"note not found: zz\"`.\n   - `get_note {\"id\":\"n-1 \"}` returns `-32602 \"note not found\"`.\n   - SDK clients throw on these, so the model never sees the message and cannot correct itself. The newer spec revisions want input and validation problems returned as `result` with `isError: true`.\n   - Only an unknown tool name should stay a protocol error. That one currently uses -32601; -32602 is the usual code.\n   - Fix: catch `ValueError` in the `tools/call` branch of `handle` and return `tool_result(msg, is_error=True)`.\n\n2. **Auth failures use a bare HTTP 401 with no OAuth hints.**\n   - Anonymous `add_note` returns `401 {\"error\":\"sign in required\"}`, which is not a JSON-RPC body.\n   - Anonymous `resources/list`, `resources/read` and `resources/templates/list` also return 401.\n   - There is no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`. `GET` on that path returns 405 \"use POST\".\n   - Clients that see a 401 start OAuth discovery, fail, and may flag the whole server as needs-auth, which hides the read tools that work anonymously.\n   - `tools/list` advertises `add_note` to everyone, and `initialize` advertises `resources` to everyone. Anonymous users then get a 401 on both. This contradicts the capabilities the server announces.\n   - Pick one of these:\n     - Make the anonymous resources endpoints work (public guide only).\n     - Return `isError: true` for `add_note`, with a message that says to use a bearer token.\n     - Add a proper `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` header and metadata.\n   - The \"signed-in session\" wording in the tool description and instructions is also misleading, since the real mechanism is a static bearer header.\n\n3. **Notifications get a response body they should not have.**\n   - `notifications/cancelled` (and any other notification except `initialized`) gets `200` with `{\"id\":null,\"error\":-32601}`. It should get `202` with no body, and it is logged `ok:false`.\n   - Clients send `notifications/cancelled`, `notifications/roots/list_changed` and similar routinely. Handle them with a no-op, or treat any request with no `id` as a notification.\n   - A batch containing only notifications returns `200 []` instead of `202`.\n   - Not a client problem, but a batch of `[]` is not rejected as invalid.\n\n4. **The declared schemas are not enforced, and some are weak.**\n   - `search_notes` declares `limit` min 1 and max 50. `limit: 0` is accepted and returns nothing, and `limit: \"5\"` (a string) is accepted. Values like `\"abc\"` or 999 give a -32602 error or no check at all.\n   - `get_note` declares `pattern ^n-[0-9]+$`, but a bad id falls through to \"not found\".\n   - `add_note` does not validate `title` (minLength 1). A missing `body` raises `KeyError`, which is not caught, so you get a dropped connection or a 500 instead of a clean error.\n   - `search_notes` has `outputSchema: {\"type\":\"object\"}`, which says nothing about `hits` and `total`. Declare the real shape.\n   - `total` is the count after truncating to `limit`, not the number of matches.\n   - `get_note` passes the schema: the output validated for `n-1`, and the text and `structuredContent` agree.\n\n5. **Browser and HTTP-level gaps.**\n   - `OPTIONS /mcp` returns `501` with an HTML page. There are no CORS headers, so browser-based clients and inspectors cannot connect.\n   - `GET /mcp` returns `405` with no `Allow: POST` header. That is acceptable for the spec's \"no SSE stream\" case, but add the header.\n   - The server speaks HTTP/1.0 with no keep-alive, so every request opens a new connection. It still works.\n   - `Mcp-Session-Id` is issued at `initialize` but never stored or validated. A bogus session id is accepted, and `DELETE` always returns 204. Harmless, but sessions mean nothing.\n   - The `MCP-Protocol-Version` request header is ignored. `1999-01-01` was accepted.\n   - A request that sends `Accept: text/event-stream` only still gets JSON.\n\n6. **`resources/read` ignores the URI.** `notes://other` returns the guide content labelled `notes://guide`. It should return an error for an unknown URI. `resources/templates/list` is implemented, and `prompts/list` correctly returns -32601 because no prompts capability is declared.\n\n7. **Logging gaps.**\n   - Calls are logged one JSON line each, with method, tool, ok, ms and who, which is good.\n   - Batched requests are not logged at all.\n   - `who` is only `dev` or `anon`.\n   - Parse errors are not logged.\n   - Failed `tools/call` logs are `ok:false` only by inference from the body.\n\n8. **Data and concurrency, lower priority.**\n   - `add_note` computes the next id as `max(...) + 1` with no lock on a `ThreadingHTTPServer`. Concurrent writes can collide.\n   - New notes are in memory only and vanish on restart.\n   - `max()` raises on an empty store.\n\n## Passed\n\n- `initialize` and `tools/list` work with no token and return 3 tools.\n- Tool names match `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`.\n- Version negotiation: `protocolVersion: \"banana\"` gets `2025-06-18` back, not an echo. (The server always answers with its one version, which is fine.)\n- `server/discover` without a token returns `-32601`, not a 401, so probing clients are not blocked.\n- The session is not broken by an error: a normal call after a refusal works.\n- `get_note` output validates against its declared `outputSchema`, and the text and structured content agree.\n- `ping` works and the parse-error path returns -32700.\n\n## Not checked\n\n- Registry listing or a surface-fingerprint lock: README says the server is not listed, and there is no lock file in the repo. That means nothing stops the tool surface drifting unnoticed. I made no external requests.\n- `add_note` output schema validation against the real success path (only seen with the in-memory write above, where it returned `{\"id\":\"n-6\"}` and matched).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1305,"cache_read_input_tokens":35044,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1305},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"733d1f61-b9aa-4769-a690-e182734fcdea","uuid":"a0651246-4313-4f6a-854f-d4baaf4fcb1c","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:11:10.283Z","request_id":"req_011CgArzFWYtsjXFRHMH4n5L","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MVgWZwTSLydJ3cDbCVzQyX":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-GiJWSF/ANSWER.md","content":"# notes-mcp: what real MCP clients will trip over\n\nChecked live against `python3 server.py` on port 56521 (curl, plus a read of `server.py`). I changed nothing in the server.\n\n## Disclosure\n\nWhile testing I called `add_note` twice with the dev token from README.md. The first call created `n-6`, and the second was a missing-ar